# Appendix: where the mixing comes from

Tracers are transported by **advection and diffusion**
([Oceananigans boundary conditions](https://clima.github.io/OceananigansDocumentation/stable/models/boundary_conditions)).
In our horizontally homogeneous 1D columns, horizontal tracer gradients vanish
and vertical **fluid** velocity is zero. Wind-driven horizontal velocities *u* and *v*
can still evolve, but do not advect tracers horizontally. Vertical turbulent
**diffusivity** therefore describes the physical transport of dissolved tracers;
particles additionally sink.

## 1. NumericalEarth physical inputs and response

This figure was generated using [NumericalEarth.jl](https://github.com/NumericalEarth/NumericalEarth.jl)
with the [CATKE turbulence model](https://clima.github.io/OceananigansDocumentation/stable/models/turbulence_closures),
[JRA55 atmospheric forcing](https://jra.kishou.go.jp/JRA-55/index_en.html), and
[EN4 initial temperature and salinity](https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/hadobs/en4/),
following the [NumericalEarth documentation](https://numericalearth.github.io/NumericalEarthDocumentation/stable/).
It shows the wind-driven current response and hydrography in a 1D ocean column.

